[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"SOLID Principles in Core Java"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"SOLID is an acronym representing five design principles intended to make software designs more understandable, flexible, and maintainable. These principles are especially relevant in object-oriented programming, like Java. Applying SOLID principles helps in creating robust and scalable applications."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Single Responsibility Principle states that a class should have only one reason to change. In other words, a class should have only one job or responsibility. This promotes high cohesion and low coupling."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"// Before applying SRP
class User {
    public void changePassword(String newPassword) { /*...*/ }
    public void generateReport() { /*...*/ }
}

// After applying SRP
class User {
    public void changePassword(String newPassword) { /*...*/ }
}

class UserReportGenerator {
    public void generateReport(User user) { /*...*/ }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the 'before' example, the `User` class is responsible for both user management (changing password) and report generation. This violates SRP. In the 'after' example, the report generation responsibility is moved to a separate `UserReportGenerator` class, adhering to SRP."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Open/Closed Principle (OCP)"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Open/Closed Principle states that software entities (classes, modules, functions, etc.) should be open for extension, but closed for modification. This means you should be able to add new functionality without modifying existing code."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"// Before applying OCP
class Rectangle {
    public double width;
    public double height;
}

class Circle {
    public double radius;
}

class AreaCalculator {
    public double calculateArea(Object shape) {
        if (shape instanceof Rectangle) {
            Rectangle rectangle = (Rectangle) shape;
            return rectangle.width * rectangle.height;
        } else if (shape instanceof Circle) {
            Circle circle = (Circle) shape;
            return Math.PI * circle.radius * circle.radius;
        }
        return 0;
    }
}

// After applying OCP
interface Shape {
    double calculateArea();
}

class Rectangle implements Shape {
    public double width;
    public double height;

    @Override
    public double calculateArea() {
        return width * height;
    }
}

class Circle implements Shape {
    public double radius;

    @Override
    public double calculateArea() {
        return Math.PI * radius * radius;
    }
}

class AreaCalculator {
    public double calculateArea(Shape shape) {
        return shape.calculateArea();
    }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the 'before' example, adding a new shape would require modifying the `AreaCalculator` class, violating OCP. In the 'after' example, we introduce an interface `Shape` with a `calculateArea()` method. Each shape class implements this interface. Adding a new shape only requires creating a new class that implements the `Shape` interface, without modifying `AreaCalculator`."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Liskov Substitution Principle states that subtypes must be substitutable for their base types without altering the correctness of the program. In simpler terms, if you have a class hierarchy, you should be able to use any subclass wherever you use the parent class without causing unexpected behavior."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"// Before applying LSP
class Bird {
    public void fly() {
        System.out.println("Bird is flying");
    }
}

class Ostrich extends Bird {
    @Override
    public void fly() {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Ostrich cannot fly");
    }
}

// After applying LSP
interface FlyingBird {
    void fly();
}

class Bird {
    // Common bird properties and methods
}

class Sparrow extends Bird implements FlyingBird {
    @Override
    public void fly() {
        System.out.println("Sparrow is flying");
    }
}

class Ostrich extends Bird {
    // Ostrich specific properties and methods
}"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the 'before' example, `Ostrich` extends `Bird` but cannot fly. This violates LSP because substituting a `Bird` with an `Ostrich` will cause the `fly()` method to throw an exception, leading to unexpected behavior. In the 'after' example, we introduce a `FlyingBird` interface. Only birds that can fly implement this interface. `Ostrich` now only extends `Bird` and does not implement `FlyingBird`, adhering to LSP."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Interface Segregation Principle states that clients should not be forced to depend on methods they do not use. In other words, a large interface should be split into smaller, more specific interfaces so that clients only need to know about the methods that are of interest to them."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"// Before applying ISP
interface Worker {
    void work();
    void eat();
}

class Human implements Worker {
    @Override
    public void work() { /*...*/ }

    @Override
    public void eat() { /*...*/ }
}

class Robot implements Worker {
    @Override
    public void work() { /*...*/ }

    @Override
    public void eat() { // Robots don't eat, but must implement this
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Robots don't eat");
    }
}

// After applying ISP
interface Workable {
    void work();
}

interface Eatable {
    void eat();
}

class Human implements Workable, Eatable {
    @Override
    public void work() { /*...*/ }

    @Override
    public void eat() { /*...*/ }
}

class Robot implements Workable {
    @Override
    public void work() { /*...*/ }
}"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the 'before' example, the `Worker` interface forces `Robot` to implement the `eat()` method, even though robots don't eat. This violates ISP. In the 'after' example, we split the `Worker` interface into `Workable` and `Eatable` interfaces. `Robot` only implements `Workable`, adhering to ISP."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Dependency Inversion Principle states that high-level modules should not depend on low-level modules. Both should depend on abstractions (interfaces or abstract classes). Secondly, abstractions should not depend on details. Details (concrete implementations) should depend on abstractions."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"// Before applying DIP
class LightBulb {
    public void turnOn() { /*...*/ }
    public void turnOff() { /*...*/ }
}

class Switch {
    private LightBulb bulb = new LightBulb();

    public void operate() {
        bulb.turnOn();
    }
}

// After applying DIP
interface Switchable {
    void turnOn();
    void turnOff();
}

class LightBulb implements Switchable {
    @Override
    public void turnOn() { /*...*/ }

    @Override
    public void turnOff() { /*...*/ }
}

class Switch {
    private Switchable device;

    public Switch(Switchable device) {
        this.device = device;
    }

    public void operate() {
        device.turnOn();
    }
}"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the 'before' example, `Switch` depends directly on `LightBulb`, a concrete implementation. This violates DIP. In the 'after' example, we introduce a `Switchable` interface. `Switch` now depends on the `Switchable` interface, and `LightBulb` implements this interface. This allows us to easily switch to a different type of device (e.g., a fan) without modifying the `Switch` class, adhering to DIP."},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"By adhering to SOLID principles, you can create more maintainable, flexible, and robust Java applications. These principles promote loose coupling, high cohesion, and easier testing, leading to better overall software design."}]
